; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Pizza Toppings and Coin Sums
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, subsets, lists, choices, search
; SUMMARY:  Lists every possible choice of pizza toppings by deciding yes or no for each one, then finds which coins add up to an amount.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   With the toppings [cheese olives ham], you can order any SELECTION
;   of them, including none or all. For the first topping there are
;   two choices, leave it off or put it on, and in both cases the rest
;   of the toppings can be chosen in every possible way. So SUBSETS
;   finds the subsets of the rest once, and outputs them twice: once as
;   they are, and once with the first topping added to each.
;   The second part uses the same yes or no idea to find every handful
;   of coins from a purse that adds up to exactly some amount.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and read the menu and the coin answers.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line
;        localmake "without subsets butfirst :items
;      and run. Press F5 a few times: each SUBSETS on the Call Stack
;      holds one fewer topping.
;   3. In COINSUM, set a breakpoint on  if :target = 0 [print :chosen stop]
;      with the condition  :target = 0  and the debugger stops only when
;      an answer has just been found. Look at :chosen.
;   4. Change the purse or the amount at the end of the file.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a yes/no choice for each item gives 2 x 2 x 2 ... possibilities
;   * reusing one recursive result twice instead of computing it again
;   * searching by recursion and printing answers as they are found
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Add a fifth topping. How many orders are there now? A sixth?
;   * Only print orders with exactly two toppings, using FILTER.
;   * Change COINSUM so it counts the answers instead of printing them.
; ============================================================

to subsets :items
  if emptyp :items [output [[]]]
  localmake "without subsets butfirst :items
  output sentence :without map [fput first :items ?] :without
end

; Print the orders as a numbered menu. An empty list means plain pizza.
to menu :toppings
  localmake "n 0
  foreach subsets :toppings [
    make "n :n + 1
    ifelse emptyp ? [print (sentence (word :n ".) [plain dough])] [
      print (sentence (word :n ".) [pizza with] ?)
    ]
  ]
end

; Try to make :target from the coins still in :purse. :chosen holds the
; coins picked so far, so when :target reaches 0 it is an answer.
to coinsum :purse :target :chosen
  if :target = 0 [print :chosen stop]
  if emptyp :purse [stop]
  if :target < 0 [stop]
  ; YES: use the first coin
  coinsum butfirst :purse :target - first :purse lput first :purse :chosen
  ; NO: skip it
  coinsum butfirst :purse :target :chosen
end

print [Every pizza you can order:]
menu [cheese olives ham mushroom]
print []
print (sentence count subsets [a b c d e f] [ways to choose from six toppings])
print []
print [Coins from the purse [50 25 20 10 5 2 1] that make 37:]
coinsum [50 25 20 10 5 2 1] 37 []
